+# CLI Contract: `tarx create`
+
+**Feature**: `002-create-command` | **Date**: 2026-11-05 | **Spec**: [../spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+This document extends the project's only external interface, the `tarx` command line, with a third
+subcommand. It is normative: the test suite asserts against these exact strings and statuses, and
+nothing outside this document and
+[001's contract](../../001-tar-archive-cli/contracts/cli-contract.md) is part of the public surface.
+
+[001's contract](../../001-tar-archive-cli/contracts/cli-contract.md) remains in force **verbatim**
+for `list` and `extract`. FR-049 and SC-015 forbid any change to it, including to the diagnostics
+table, the exit statuses, and the validation order recorded there.
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m tarx list    ARCHIVE
+python -m tarx extract ARCHIVE DEST [--overwrite]
+python -m tarx create  ARCHIVE PATH... [--overwrite]      # new in this feature
+```
+
+- Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH` (the package lives at `src/tarx/`).
+- `prog` remains `tarx`, so usage text reads `usage: tarx ...` (001 research R7). Adding a third
+  subparser does not change the top-level usage line, which uses `metavar="COMMAND"`.
+- Exactly three subcommands now exist; all are spelled in full (FR-029).
+- No environment variables, config files, or stdin input affect behavior (FR-029 … FR-056).
+
+## Command: `create ARCHIVE PATH... [--overwrite]`
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Positionals | `ARCHIVE` — the archive to write; then one or more `PATH` arguments (`nargs="+"`) (FR-030) |
+| Options | `--overwrite` — replace an existing regular file at `ARCHIVE` (FR-044) |
+| stdout | exactly one line: `created ARCHIVE with N files\n` (FR-038) |
+| `ARCHIVE` in that line | the archive path **exactly as typed**, unnormalised (FR-038) |
+| `N` | decimal count of regular-file members stored (FR-039) |
+| Word after `N` | the literal `files`, for every `N` including `0` and `1` (FR-039) |
+| stderr on success | empty (FR-038) |
+| Exit on success | `0` (FR-038) |
+
+### What is stored
+
+- Each `PATH` naming a regular file → one regular-file member, content byte-identical (FR-032).
+- Each `PATH` naming a directory → a directory member for it, plus a member for every entry at any
+  depth beneath it (FR-033). An empty directory is stored as a directory member, so it survives a
+  create-then-extract round trip.
+- Recorded member names are the normalised relative path: no leading `./`, no absolute prefix, `/`
+  as the separator (FR-034). Directory headers carry tar's conventional trailing `/`, which
+  `list` and every other tar reader strip or display per their own convention — see
+  [research R17](../research.md#r17--directory-member-names-the-trailing-separator-and-the-archive-format).
+- Entries that are neither regular files nor directories (symbolic links, FIFOs, device nodes,
+  sockets) are **skipped** — not stored, not counted, not an error — whether named directly or met
+  while walking (FR-036).
+- The archive being written is never stored inside itself, even when `ARCHIVE` falls inside a named
+  directory (FR-037).
+- No de-duplication: a path named twice, or a directory and a file inside it both named, stores the
+  overlapping entries more than once and counts each stored regular file.
+
+### Member order
+
+Deterministic for a given invocation and tree (FR-035):
+
+1. `PATH` arguments in command-line order.
+2. Within a directory: its own member first, then its entries in `sorted()` (code-point) order,
+   files and subdirectories interleaved by name.
+
+### Compression
+
+| `ARCHIVE` ends in (case-insensitive) | Written as |
+|---|---|
+| `.tar.gz`, `.tgz` | gzip-compressed tar (FR-040) |
+| anything else, including no suffix | plain, uncompressed tar (FR-041) |
+
+The suffix of the typed archive path is the **only** input to this choice; the names and contents of
+the `PATH` arguments are irrelevant (FR-042). This is deliberately asymmetric with `list` and
+`extract`, which sniff compression from content (FR-018) — and harmless, because a content-sniffing
+reader accepts a name-driven writer's output.
+
+### Interoperability
+
+| Guarantee | Requirement |
+|---|---|
+| Every archive `create` writes, compressed or not, is readable by `list`, whose output is the recorded member names in archive order | FR-047 |
+| Every archive `create` writes is extractable by `extract`, reproducing each regular file at its recorded relative path with byte-identical content and each directory as a directory | FR-048 |
+| `list` and `extract` behave **identically** to before this feature, for every input — FR-001 … FR-028 all still hold | FR-049 |
+
+**Scope restriction on FR-048's count equality** — `extract`'s reported count equals `create`'s
+reported count whenever the named `PATH` arguments do not overlap. When they do overlap, `create`
+counts each stored member while `extract` counts each distinct destination path written (FR-015), so
+`create` reports the larger number. This is a specification-internal conflict resolved in the plan;
+see [research R22](../research.md#r22--counting-regularfile-members-and-the-fr048-countequality-ambiguity).
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+Used consistently and exclusively across all three subcommands (FR-056, FR-028):
+
+| Status | Meaning for `create` |
+|---|---|
+| `0` | Success — the archive was written and the summary line printed |
+| `2` | Every failure except an existing archive path without `--overwrite`: missing source path, source path outside the current directory, archive path that is a directory, archive location unwritable, unreadable source, mid-write error, invocation error |
+| `3` | `ARCHIVE` already exists and `--overwrite` was not given |
+
+Status `3` is reserved for the one condition that re-running with `--overwrite` resolves. When a
+status-2 condition and the status-3 condition are both present, **status 2 is reported** (FR-056) —
+the same rule 001 adopted for `extract` (001 research R8).
+
+### Validation order within `create`
+
+```text
+1. argv parses: a subcommand, an ARCHIVE, at least one PATH, no unknown option  → else usage, 2
+2. every PATH is relative, stays inside the current directory, and is not "."   → else 2
+3. every PATH exists                                                            → else 2
+4. ARCHIVE does not exist as a directory                                        → else 2
+5. ARCHIVE does not exist, or --overwrite was given                             → else 3
+6. if ARCHIVE exists and --overwrite was given, it is a regular file            → else 2
+───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── nothing above touches a file
+7. ARCHIVE opens for writing (created or truncated here)                        → else 2
+8. members are walked, classified, streamed, and counted                        → else 2
+9. summary line on stdout, exit 0
+```
+
+Steps 1–6 touch no files, so any failure in them leaves the filesystem exactly as it was: **no new
+archive, and any pre-existing archive byte-for-byte unmodified** (FR-050, FR-051). This matters —
+`tarfile.open(path, "w")` truncates immediately, so a design that opened the archive before
+validating would destroy the user's archive in precisely the case FR-050 protects
+([research R18](../research.md#r18--all-validation-must-precede-opening-the-archive)).
+
+Step 4 precedes step 5 because status 2 outranks status 3 (FR-045 with FR-056). Steps 2–3 precede
+step 4 for the same reason, so a run with both a bad `PATH` and an existing `ARCHIVE` reports the
+`PATH` and exits 2.
+
+A failure at step 7 or 8 leaves a partial or truncated archive on disk, but **never** prints the
+summary line — the run is reported as a failure, which is what FR-053 requires ("MUST NOT report
+success"). No temporary file or rollback is used; see
+[research R21](../research.md#r21--failure-partway-through-writing-no-temporary-file-no-rollback).
+
+## Diagnostics
+
+Every failure writes a short, human-readable, **single-line** diagnostic to stderr naming the
+offending path, and writes nothing to stdout (FR-055). No failure is signalled by exit status alone,
+and no internal traceback ever reaches the user.
+
+Format, unchanged from 001: `tarx: <message>` on one line.
+
+| Condition | Message | Status | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Source path is absolute, or reaches outside the current directory | `refusing to add '<path>' outside the current directory` | 2 | FR-052 |
+| Source path is `.` or empty | `refusing to add '<path>': not an entry inside the current directory` | 2 | FR-052 / R19 |
+| Source path does not exist | `cannot add '<path>': No such file or directory` | 2 | FR-050 |
+| Source path exists but cannot be read | `cannot add '<path>': Permission denied` | 2 | FR-054 |
+| Other error while adding a source path | `cannot add '<path>': <reason>` | 2 | FR-053, FR-054 |
+| `ARCHIVE` exists as a directory | `cannot write archive '<archive>': is a directory` | 2 | FR-045 |
+| `ARCHIVE` exists, `--overwrite` given, not a regular file | `cannot replace archive '<archive>': not a regular file` | 2 | FR-044 / R20 |
+| `ARCHIVE`'s containing directory does not exist | `cannot write archive '<archive>': No such file or directory` | 2 | FR-053 |
+| `ARCHIVE` location not writable | `cannot write archive '<archive>': Permission denied` | 2 | FR-053 |
+| Other error while writing the archive | `cannot write archive '<archive>': <reason>` | 2 | FR-053 |
+| `ARCHIVE` exists and `--overwrite` not given | `'<archive>' already exists; use --overwrite to replace it` | 3 | FR-043 |
+
+Conventions, carried over from 001's contract:
+
+- `<path>` and `<archive>` are shown **as typed on the command line**, never normalised or resolved,
+  so the message is actionable without the user re-deriving it.
+- `<reason>` is the operating system's `strerror` text, capitalised as the OS supplies it (e.g.
+  `No such file or directory`) — matching 001's `cannot write to destination '<path>': <reason>`.
+- During the write pass, an `OSError` is attributed to `exc.filename` when the OS supplies one, and
+  rendered with the archive message when that filename is the archive path, otherwise with the
+  `cannot add` message.
+- The "already exists" wording is character-for-character the same as `extract`'s overwrite
+  conflict, so the two commands' recovery advice reads identically.
+
+Invocation errors — no `PATH` given, an unknown option, a missing `ARCHIVE` — produce `argparse`'s
+own usage guidance on stderr and exit `2`, with no other action taken (FR-031). This is `argparse`'s
+default behavior and is therefore not restated in the table above.
+
+## Out of contract
+
+Not provided by `create`, and asserted nowhere: compression formats other than gzip; exclusion,
+inclusion, or glob filtering of entries; appending to or updating an existing archive; writing the
+archive to stdout or reading source data from stdin; following symbolic links; preserving or
+reproducing permissions, ownership, or modification times as a *guarantee* (whatever `tarfile`
+records is incidental and unasserted); de-duplicating overlapping source paths; creating the
+archive's containing directory; progress output, per-file listing, colors, headers, or any total
+beyond the single summary line; more than one archive per invocation; non-local destinations.
